# c36 -- write skew whose one edge comes from a read the session's
# read pin would answer with an iterator it already has.
#
# SM1_DESIGN.md §10.1: a serializable read takes its SIREAD before
# its iterator exists. T1 reads R1, which leaves the session's
# read pin holding an iterator. T2 then writes R2, in flight, with
# no SIREAD on R2 for its write to find. T1 reads R2: an iterator
# from before T2's write would not hold T2's version, so T1 would
# step past nothing and owe T2 no edge. T2 reads R3 and T1 writes
# it, the edge T2 --rw--> T1; with T1 --rw--> T2 from R2 the cycle
# closes, and under S+S one of them must fail. So a serializable
# read never seeks an iterator it already has.


== pairing RR/RR == T1=RR T2=RR
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 read R1 -> 0
@3 T2 write R2 t2 -> ok
@4 T1 read R2 -> 0
@5 T2 read R3 -> 0
@6 T1 write R3 t1 -> ok
@7 T1 commit? -> recorded
@7 T2 commit? -> recorded
@8 check committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
== pairing RR/S == T1=RR T2=SER
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 read R1 -> 0
@3 T2 write R2 t2 -> ok
@4 T1 read R2 -> 0
@5 T2 read R3 -> 0
@6 T1 write R3 t1 -> ok
@7 T1 commit? -> recorded
@7 T2 commit? -> recorded
@8 check committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
== pairing S/RR == T1=SER T2=RR
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 read R1 -> 0
@3 T2 write R2 t2 -> ok
@4 T1 read R2 -> 0
@5 T2 read R3 -> 0
@6 T1 write R3 t1 -> ok
@7 T1 commit? -> recorded
@7 T2 commit? -> recorded
@8 check committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
== pairing S/S == T1=SER T2=SER
@1 T1 begin -> ok
@1 T2 begin -> ok
@2 T1 read R1 -> 0
@3 T2 write R2 t2 -> ok
@4 T1 read R2 -> 0
@5 T2 read R3 -> 0
@6 T1 write R3 t1 -> ok
@7 T1 commit? -> recorded
@7 T2 commit? -> recorded
@8 check at-most-one-committed T1 T2 -> satisfied
